<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Hamath</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 BCE – 720 BCE (approx.) · capital Hamath</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hamath, a Syrian kingdom on the Orontes whose kings led resistance to Assyria at Qarqar and left both Luwian and Aramaic inscriptions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hamath, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, rose after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a> as a kingdom on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes</a>. Its early rulers wrote in Luwian hieroglyphs; carved stones from the city, noticed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a> in 1812, were the first Hittite hieroglyphs known to modern scholars.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urhilina">Urhilina</a>, Hamath joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aram-damascus">Aram-Damascus</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar">Qarqar</a> in 853 BCE. Around 800 BCE the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakkur">Zakkur</a> ruled Hamath and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/luash">Lu'ash</a>, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> stele tells how the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalshamin">Baalshamin</a> saved him from a siege by a coalition led by Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hamath became a tributary of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>. In 720 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yau-bidi">Yau-bidi</a> led a revolt; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar">Qarqar</a>, flayed him, and annexed Hamath as a province, deporting many of its people.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hamath.jpg" alt="Hamath" width="320" height="179"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hama,_Panoramic_view,_Syria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 BCE – 720 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hamath</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Urhilina, Zakkur, Yau-bidi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Syrian and Luwian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luwian, Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Hamath, Hamath and Lu'ash</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-910&amp;focus=hamath" aria-label="Hamath on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5611 906 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hamath lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5931 1162L6092 1134"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5931 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5931 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-910&amp;focus=hamath">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

